  "query_preview": "..Utah and southwest Wyoming remain under a moist,\nunstable, and breezy southerly flow this evening in between high\npressure over the central United States and a broad low over the\nCalifornia coast. Satellite derived PWs are in the 0.7 to 1.0 inch\nrange across the area, with the highest values over southeast\nUtah. The position of the upper jet on the front edge of the low\nhas brought the best divergence roughly to northeastern portions \nof the area with a weak wave providing additional instability,\nparticularly to areas north of I-70 and east of I-15, with one\nstorm producing ping pong ball to golf ball sized hail in the city\nof Midway. \n\nOverall, the current round of showers and thunderstorms is on the\ndecrease with a lack of daytime heating, with just a few stronger\ncells remaining. As a result, have canceled the going Flood Watch\nfor Flash Flooding as the threat has ended. However, as the low \nslides into Nevada and brings an initial boundary through, showers\nare expected to light up during the early morning, focused over \nnorthern Utah and southwest Wyoming. The low is then on track to \ngraze northern portions of the area as it moves by through \nSaturday. As a result, looking at the most widespread convection \nto be on Friday afternoon in association with the main trough and \nassociated cold front. CAMs continue to indicate the best coverage\nto be over northern portions of the area, but a few storms over \nsouthern Utah could bring an isolated threat of flash flooding. \n\nThe passage of the system will also have a noticeable effect on\ntemperatures, with highs Friday into the weekend running 5-10F\nbelow seasonal normals for northern Utah and southwest Wyoming,\nand near seasonal normals for southern Utah. As the trough exits,\nthe flow aloft will move to a more west to northwesterly\ndirection, allowing some drier air to move in. With the trough not\nentirely out of the picture on Saturday, still anticipating some\nscattered convection focused over northeastern portions of the\nforecast area, with Sunday expected to be dry with weak ridging\nmoving in. \n\nGuidance indicates the break in the action will be short lived,\nhowever, with yet another low approaching the Pacific coast Sunday\nafternoon. Most guidance shows this system tracking eastward into\nthe Desert Southwest to start the work week, but the ensembles\ndiverge noticeably with the regard to the potential track of the\nsystem and how much it will weaken once onshore. Still, confidence\nis increasing with regard to a relatively quick return to\nunsettled weather, at least for southern Utah.",
